From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 18:48:59 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] msm8992: let the GPU governor react to UI, not just sustained
 3D

msm-adreno-tz defers to TrustZone for ordinary scaling and keeps one path of its own: an accumulated
busy window that jumps straight to the top power level. That path is gated on constants chosen for a
game holding the GPU flat out -- a sample >95% busy, a frame longer than 25ms, and 50ms of
accumulated busy time.

Compositing a UI never looks like that. Measured on this device over 30 seconds of continuous
interaction: 69.7% of the time at 180MHz, 16.6% at 300MHz, 13.7% at 367MHz, and 0% at 450, 490 and
600MHz -- ten transitions in thirty seconds, while dropping frames. The TZ path does climb, but it
stops at 367MHz; the jump-to-max never armed once, so the top half of the range was unreachable
during exactly the workload that needed it.

Retune the three constants that gate that path: a sample counts at 80% busy rather than 95%, a
"long" frame becomes one 60Hz vsync interval (16666us) rather than 25ms, and the accumulated window
drops to 25ms -- still longer than a standard frame, so steady 60fps content will not trip it, and
still well under the 80ms idle timer.

This leaves the minimum at 180MHz. The complaint was never that the floor was too low; it was that
the GPU would not climb when it needed to.
---
 drivers/devfreq/governor_msm_adreno_tz.c | 25 +++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/devfreq/governor_msm_adreno_tz.c b/drivers/devfreq/governor_msm_adreno_tz.c
index e4337d19361..2fa7f968073 100644
--- a/drivers/devfreq/governor_msm_adreno_tz.c
+++ b/drivers/devfreq/governor_msm_adreno_tz.c
@@ -39,16 +39,31 @@ static DEFINE_SPINLOCK(tz_lock);
  * Use BUSY_BIN to check for fully busy rendering
  * intervals that may need early intervention when
  * seen with LONG_FRAME lengths
+ *
+ * Retuned for UI rather than sustained 3D. Upstream wanted a sample >95% busy and a frame longer
+ * than 25ms before it would count an interval as needing help, which suits a game holding the GPU
+ * flat out. Compositing a launcher does not look like that: it is short bursts well under 95%, so
+ * the early-intervention path never armed. Measured on this panel over 30s of continuous
+ * interaction, the GPU spent 69.7% of its time at 180MHz, 16.6% at 300MHz, 13.7% at 367MHz and
+ * NEVER reached 450, 490 or 600MHz -- 10 transitions in 30 seconds, while visibly dropping frames.
+ *
+ * 16666us is one 60Hz vsync interval: a frame that takes longer than that has already missed, which
+ * is the signal we actually want to react to.
  */
-#define BUSY_BIN		95
-#define LONG_FRAME		25000
+#define BUSY_BIN		80
+#define LONG_FRAME		16666
 #define MAX_TZ_VERSION		0
 
 /*
- * CEILING is 50msec, larger than any standard
- * frame length, but less than the idle timer.
+ * CEILING is the accumulated busy time that forces a jump straight to the top power level.
+ *
+ * Was 50msec. The comment's reasoning still holds -- it must stay below the 80ms idle timer -- but
+ * 50ms of accumulated busy time is roughly three frames' worth of solid GPU work, and bursty UI
+ * rendering reaches that so rarely that the jump effectively never happened. 25msec is still longer
+ * than one standard frame, so steady 60fps content will not trip it, while a sustained burst that
+ * is actually falling behind now gets the clock it needs.
  */
-#define CEILING			50000
+#define CEILING			25000
 #define TZ_RESET_ID		0x3
 #define TZ_UPDATE_ID		0x4
 #define TZ_INIT_ID		0x6
